<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <title><%= content_for(:title) || "Acme" %></title>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
    <meta name="application-name" content="Acme">

    <%# The chat widget reads this token and sends it as X-CSRF-Token, which is
        the whole reason the engine's chat endpoint needs a host-rendered page. %>
    <%= csrf_meta_tags %>
    <%= csp_meta_tag %>

    <%= yield :head %>

    <link rel="icon" href="/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag "application" %>
    <%= javascript_include_tag "turbo.min", type: "module" %>
  </head>

  <%# Turbo is here for exactly one thing: Streams, so in-app delivery can
      ACTIVELY surface instead of waiting for the customer to reload (design
      §3.5). Drive is off — `data-turbo="false"` on the body covers every link
      and form inside it.

      That is deliberate rather than lazy. Turning Drive on would change how
      every form and link in this app behaves (303 redirects, method-preserving
      follows) including the ones that navigate into the mounted engine admin,
      where a Drive visit leaves Turbo loaded on pages built without it. None of
      that buys the demo anything: the story here is "the agent's answer arrives
      on its own", and that is a Stream, not a navigation. %>
  <body data-turbo="false">
    <%# One stream per account, signed by Turbo, rendered from the session's own
        tenant and never from a parameter. It is in the layout rather than on the
        inbox page because the badge has to be able to go up while the customer
        is somewhere else entirely. %>
    <%= turbo_stream_from current_tenant if signed_in? %>

    <%= render "shared/topbar" if signed_in? %>

    <main class="page">
      <%= render "shared/flash" %>
      <%= yield %>
    </main>

    <%= render "shared/chat_widget" if signed_in? %>
  </body>
</html>
